even though oil prices have started to  come down a bit due to fears of a  recession they're still more than double  what they were two years ago and while  high prices are a problem for many  global economies they've actually been a  boon for opec plus countries here's how  opec plus members are benefiting from  russia's war in ukraine and how opec  plus decisions have given russia a  buffer against sanctions  first let's look at who makes up opec  plus and why high oil prices are so  important to them opec plus includes the  13 members of the saudi-led organization  of the petroleum exporting countries  otherwise known as opec plus 10 non-opec  producers led by russia together these  countries control more than half of the  world's oil output and while there are  disputes and sometimes failure to follow  through on commitments they generally  work together to exert control over the  price of crude as one analyst i talked  to put it coordination is king some oil  experts say that opec plus has a  so-called goldilocks objective they  don't want oil prices too hot since that  can tip the world into a recession but  they also don't want oil prices too cold  because many of these countries rely on  revenue from oil to balance their  budgets and pay for things like social  services and military spending even  though the key architect of the opec  plus alliance mohammed barkindo recently  passed away his efforts are likely to  continue impacting oil producers and  consumers for years to come  when the pandemic hit falling demand for  oil caused the opec plus alliance to  briefly crack and oil prices to collapse  battering many opec plus countries  so when the world opened back up and  demand for oil bounced back opec plus  countries agreed to start pumping more  oil together but at a slow and steady  pace to support prices that slow and  steady pace frustrated some global  leaders like president biden as oil  prices surged the white house is  confronting the spike in gas prices by  telling opec it must do more to support  global production then russia invaded  ukraine and oil prices soared even  higher the price of oil surged over 100  a barrel in hours after russia launched  the attack while many western nations  slapped sanctions on russia and cut ties  with the kremlin opec plus leaders  continued to honor that slow and steady  deal they struck with moscow despite  pressure from the u.s at the time saudi  and emirati leaders declined calls with  president biden as he was trying to  contain a surge in oil prices and  alienate russia since russia's oil  revenues are used to finance its war  everybody leaves his politics at the  outside door of that building  so now we have high oil prices and those  high oil prices are benefiting opec plus  countries here are five reasons why  first their economies are growing while  the war in ukraine is dealing an  economic setback for much of the world  the imf estimates that oil producers in  the middle east and north africa will  receive an additional 320 billion  dollars more than expected this year  some countries like iraq and algeria are  using the extra cash to help their  citizens boosting pensions expanding  benefits and passing laws to mitigate  rising costs other opec plus members  like saudi arabia and the uae are  spending billions of dollars abroad to  project power and enhance their security  interests in recent months riyadh and  abu dhabi have struck investment deals  and approved aid packages to the tune of  billions of dollars to help regional  neighbors like pakistan turkey and egypt  and according to analysts i spoke to  high oil prices might even help lead to  a revived nuclear deal with iran another  opec plus member since the country could  bring up to 1.5 million barrels a day of  oil to the market and finally another  opec plus member that has benefited from  high oil prices russia even though  russia isn't producing as much oil as it  normally would because of sanctions high  oil prices are helping it make more  money on the oil that it is selling  according to the center for research on  energy and clean air in the first 100  days of russia's war it raked in 97  billion dollars from fossil fuel exports  so why does all of this matter because  oil prices affect all of us but also  because russia continues to use oil  revenue to finance its war some opec  leaders are saying that even if they  wanted to pump more oil they can't  they're reaching max capacity now there  are fears of a recession and some  investors think slowing economic growth  could push demand for oil lower once  again and when demand falls prices  usually do too that's why some analysts  say that opec plus isn't likely to kick  russia out of the alliance or  dramatically ramp up pressure on moscow  because opec plus will ultimately need  russia's cooperation in order to control  oil prices in the future speaking of oil  prices they are sure to be a hot topic  when president biden visits saudi arabia  later this month check out this video  for a preview of the talks thanks for  watching  [Music]  you
 

